WEBVTT

00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:05.620
Welcome to the Microsoft for Education Daily, Friday edition.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.920
Here's what shipped at Microsoft in the last 24 hours.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.060
Time to tidy up the office inbox: Copilot Chat is

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:21.310
rolling out to shared mailboxes in classic Outlook.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:26.840
Delegates get it too: run the principal's inbox, and Copilot's right there.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:32.660
For classes,

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:37.900
a page shared in a Teams chat or channel will open right in Teams, due January.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:41.690
Edit it together and talk it through in t

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:44.000
he same window: handy for group projects.

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:49.080
Teams is also getting more Markdown in the compose box,

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:50.900
with a preview due in December.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:57.600
Good news for coding classes: format messages the way you'd write a README file.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.800
School IT building lab PCs?

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:06.880
WinGet's new release candidate can keep installing when one app is missing.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.690
And when one name matches several apps,

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:12.390
an experimental prompt lets you pick by number.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:16.830
Building AI agents on campus?

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:21.310
Microsoft's new guide shows how to keep Copilot Studio costs in check.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:25.250
Makers see estimated credits as they build,

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:28.190
and admins can cap each agent and get alerts.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.720
Something calmer for the finance office: Copilot in Excel can now reconcile accounts,

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:36.860
in early access.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:41.380
It drafts journal entries and explains what changed against the plan,

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:43.020
all ready for you to review.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.160
The fine print? From December 30,

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:52.410
GitHub bills Copilot code reviews to your organization, unless you pick Member.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:55.890
Also on the way out: Windows' Telnet Client,

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:59.130
and Java 8, 11 and 17 on Azure Functions.

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:03.250
After hours on Xbox,

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:07.630
Game Pass members can play Little Rocket Lab free until Sunday.

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:11.140
And GitHub's advice for coders,

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:15.410
students included: give yourself permission to play, and call it learning.

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:17.770
Four years in,

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:23.520
its conference badges have colour screens and game controls; one even became a pedometer.

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:28.520
Old tools out, budgets clear, a little play: a good week to tidy up.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.820
Every day, the changelog gets a little longer.

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:36.690
On the release board: shipped, and still shipping.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:41.410
That was 24 hours at Microsoft. Tomorrow, there's more.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.950
That's the Microsoft for Education Daily for today.

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:51.310
Stay curious. See you tomorrow.
